{"notes":[{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Anonymous Preprint Submission"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e7213794106b104fc2ce9f22acdd2b948c073984.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Transformer Architecture","Attention Head Pruning","Model Stability","Attention Entropy"]},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint"},"abstract":{"value":"[Rebuttal Material] Entropy Meets Importance: A Unified Head Importance–Entropy Score for Stable and Efficient Transformer Pruning"},"title":{"value":"Rebuttal Material - Entropy Meets Importance: A Unified Head Importance–Entropy Score for Stable and Efficient Transformer Pruning"}},"tmdate":1774927917204,"tcdate":1774855228827,"writers":["OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint","OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint/Submission757/Authors"],"signatures":["OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint/Submission757/Authors"],"forum":"Say8k6wrvs","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":757,"cdate":1774855228827,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint/-/Submission"],"mdate":1774927917204,"odate":1774927917204,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint","id":"Say8k6wrvs","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Anonymous Preprint Submission"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/81f2f09f7d19d5a9dc2de7da5461c3b183172aed.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Transformer Architecture","Attention Head Pruning","Model Stability","Attention Entropy"]},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint"},"abstract":{"value":"[Rebuttal Material] Entropy Meets Importance: A Unified Head Importance–Entropy Score for Stable and Efficient Transformer Pruning"},"title":{"value":"Rebuttal Material [Updated] - Entropy Meets Importance: A Unified Head Importance–Entropy Score for Stable and Efficient Transformer Pruning"}},"tmdate":1775270062218,"tcdate":1775270062218,"writers":["OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint","OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint/Submission763/Authors"],"signatures":["OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint/Submission763/Authors"],"forum":"8tWnmFSaxp","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":763,"cdate":1775270062218,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint/-/Submission"],"mdate":1775270062218,"odate":1775270062218,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Anonymous_Preprint","id":"8tWnmFSaxp","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Entropy 2022"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1099-4300/24/3/379/pdf?version=1646734537"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ENTROPY/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"yan|entropy_analysis_of_heart_rate_variability_in_different_sleep_stages"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","","~Jianqing_Li2","",""]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/e24030379"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/entropy/YanLYLLZL22,\n  author={Chang Yan and Peng Li and Meicheng Yang and Yang Li and Jianqing Li and Hongxing Zhang and Chengyu Liu},\n  title={Entropy Analysis of Heart Rate Variability in Different Sleep Stages},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  journal={Entropy},\n  volume={24},\n  number={3},\n  pages={379},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/e24030379}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"How the complexity or irregularity of heart rate variability (HRV) changes across different sleep stages and the importance of these features in sleep staging are not fully understood. This study aimed to investigate the complexity or irregularity of the RR interval time series in different sleep stages and explore their values in sleep staging. We performed approximate entropy (ApEn), sample entropy (SampEn), fuzzy entropy (FuzzyEn), distribution entropy (DistEn), conditional entropy (CE), and permutation entropy (PermEn) analyses on RR interval time series extracted from epochs that were constructed based on two methods: (1) 270-s epoch length and (2) 300-s epoch length. To test whether adding the entropy measures can improve the accuracy of sleep staging using linear HRV indices, XGBoost was used to examine the abilities to differentiate among: (i) 5 classes [Wake (W), non-rapid-eye-movement (NREM), which can be divide into 3 sub-stages: stage N1, stage N2, and stage N3, and rapid-eye-movement (REM)]; (ii) 4 classes [W, light sleep (combined N1 and N2), deep sleep (N3), and REM]; and (iii) 3 classes: (W, NREM, and REM). SampEn, FuzzyEn, and CE significantly increased from W to N3 and decreased in REM. DistEn increased from W to N1, decreased in N2, and further decreased in N3; it increased in REM. The average accuracy of the three tasks using linear and entropy features were 42.1%, 59.1%, and 60.8%, respectively, based on 270-s epoch length; all were significantly lower than the performance based on 300-s epoch length (i.e., 54.3%, 63.1%, and 67.5%, respectively). Adding entropy measures to the XGBoost model of linear parameters did not significantly improve the classification performance. However, entropy measures, especially PermEn, DistEn, and FuzzyEn, demonstrated greater importance than most of the linear parameters in the XGBoost model.300-s270-s."},"title":{"value":"Entropy Analysis of Heart Rate Variability in Different Sleep Stages"},"authors":{"value":["Chang Yan","Peng Li","Meicheng Yang","Yang Li","Jianqing Li","Hongxing Zhang","Chengyu Liu"]}},"tmdate":1774944458291,"pdate":1672444800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/entropy/YanLYLLZL22"],"tcdate":1774944431613,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jianqing_Li2"],"forum":"o4vACyGi6s","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":861536,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1774944458291,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"o4vACyGi6s","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Entropy 2023"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1099-4300/25/2/185/pdf?version=1675070381"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ENTROPY/2023"},"paperhash":{"value":"skorski|towards_more_efficient_rényi_entropy_estimation"},"authorids":{"value":["~Maciej_Skorski1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/e25020185"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/entropy/Skorski23,\n  author={Maciej Skorski},\n  title={Towards More Efficient Rényi Entropy Estimation},\n  year={2023},\n  month={February},\n  cdate={1675209600000},\n  journal={Entropy},\n  volume={25},\n  number={2},\n  pages={185},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/e25020185}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Estimation of Rényi entropy is of fundamental importance to many applications in cryptography, statistical inference, and machine learning. This paper aims to improve the existing estimators with regard to: (a) the sample size, (b) the estimator adaptiveness, and (c) the simplicity of the analyses. The contribution is a novel analysis of the generalized “birthday paradox” collision estimator. The analysis is simpler than in prior works, gives clear formulas, and strengthens existing bounds. The improved bounds are used to develop an adaptive estimation technique that outperforms previous methods, particularly in regimes of low or moderate entropy. Last but not least, to demonstrate that the developed techniques are of broader interest, a number of applications concerning theoretical and practical properties of “birthday estimators” are discussed."},"title":{"value":"Towards More Efficient Rényi Entropy Estimation"},"authors":{"value":["Maciej Skorski"]}},"tmdate":1720046218216,"pdate":1672531200000,"tcdate":1720046203889,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Maciej_Skorski1"],"forum":"QzKkxs2Ts3","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":40741,"cdate":1675209600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1720046218216,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"QzKkxs2Ts3","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Entropy 2017"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1099-4300/19/1/37/pdf?version=1484728506"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ENTROPY/2017"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|evaluation_model_of_aluminum_alloy_welded_joint_lowcycle_fatigue_data_based_on_information_entropy"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yaliang_Liu:","~Li_Zou3","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yibo_Sun:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xinhua_Yang:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/e19010037"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/entropy/LiuZSY17,\n  author={Yaliang Liu and Li Zou and Yibo Sun and Xinhua Yang},\n  title={Evaluation Model of Aluminum Alloy Welded Joint Low-Cycle Fatigue Data Based on Information Entropy},\n  year={2017},\n  cdate={1483228800000},\n  journal={Entropy},\n  volume={19},\n  number={1},\n  pages={37},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/e19010037}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"An evaluation model of aluminum alloy welded joint low-cycle fatigue data based on information entropy is proposed. Through calculating and analyzing the information entropy of decision attributes, quantitative contribution of stress concentration, plate thickness, and loading mode to the fatigue destruction are researched. Results reveal that the total information entropy of the fatigue data based on nominal stress, structural stress and equivalent structural stress are, respectively, 0.9702, 0.8881, and 0.8294. There is consistency between the reducing trend of the weight-based information entropy and the smaller and smaller standard deviation of the S-N curves. In the structural stress based S-N curve, total stress concentration factor is crucial for the distribution of the fatigue data and the weight based information entropy of membrane stress concentration factor is 0.6754, which illustrates that stress concentration is a key issue of welded structure to which ought to be attached great importance. Subsequently, in the equivalent structural stress-based S-N curve, the weight based information entropy of stress ratio is 0.5759, which plays an important role in the distribution of fatigue data. With the importance level of the attributes on the S-N curves investigated, the correction of R in the equivalent structural stress based master S-N curve method should be carried out to make the welding fatigue prediction much more accurate."},"title":{"value":"Evaluation Model of Aluminum Alloy Welded Joint Low-Cycle Fatigue Data Based on Information Entropy"},"authors":{"value":["Yaliang Liu","Li Zou","Yibo Sun","Xinhua Yang"]}},"tmdate":1744440690341,"pdate":1483228800000,"tcdate":1744440614231,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Li_Zou3"],"forum":"RpcbS7iads","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":395021,"cdate":1483228800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1744440690341,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"RpcbS7iads","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Entropy 2016"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1099-4300/18/5/189/pdf?version=1470751094"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ENTROPY/2016"},"paperhash":{"value":"yao|monbtav_mediumentropy_alloy"},"authorids":{"value":["~Hongwei_Yao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jun-Wei_Qiao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Michael_C._Gao:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jeffrey_A._Hawk:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sheng-Guo_Ma:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hefeng_Zhou:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/e18050189"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/entropy/YaoQGHMZ16,\n  author={Hongwei Yao and Jun-Wei Qiao and Michael C. Gao and Jeffrey A. Hawk and Sheng-Guo Ma and Hefeng Zhou},\n  title={MoNbTaV Medium-Entropy Alloy},\n  year={2016},\n  cdate={1451606400000},\n  journal={Entropy},\n  volume={18},\n  number={5},\n  pages={189},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/e18050189}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Guided by CALPHAD (Calculation of Phase Diagrams) modeling, the refractory medium-entropy alloy MoNbTaV was synthesized by vacuum arc melting under a high-purity argon atmosphere. A body-centered cubic solid solution phase was experimentally confirmed in the as-cast ingot using X-ray diffraction and scanning electron microscopy. The measured lattice parameter of the alloy (3.208 Å) obeys the rule of mixtures (ROM), but the Vickers microhardness (4.95 GPa) and the yield strength (1.5 GPa) are about 4.5 and 4.6 times those estimated from the ROM, respectively. Using a simple model on solid solution strengthening predicts a yield strength of approximately 1.5 GPa. Thermodynamic analysis shows that the total entropy of the alloy is more than three times the configurational entropy at room temperature, and the entropy of mixing exhibits a small negative departure from ideal mixing."},"title":{"value":"MoNbTaV Medium-Entropy Alloy"},"authors":{"value":["Hongwei Yao","Jun-Wei Qiao","Michael C. Gao","Jeffrey A. Hawk","Sheng-Guo Ma","Hefeng Zhou"]}},"tmdate":1727497046141,"pdate":1451606400000,"tcdate":1727497038253,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hongwei_Yao1"],"forum":"nvDLIjuS8W","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":98883,"cdate":1451606400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1727497046141,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"nvDLIjuS8W","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Entropy 2020"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1099-4300/22/1/20/pdf?version=1577090890"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ENTROPY/2020"},"paperhash":{"value":"liu|contextaware_superpixel_and_bilateral_entropy_image_coherence_induces_less_entropy"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Feihong_Liu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Xiao_Zhang_0028:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Hongyu_Wang_0007:","~Jun_Feng2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/e22010020"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/entropy/LiuZWF20,\n  author={Feihong Liu and Xiao Zhang and Hongyu Wang and Jun Feng},\n  title={Context-Aware Superpixel and Bilateral Entropy - Image Coherence Induces Less Entropy},\n  year={2020},\n  cdate={1577836800000},\n  journal={Entropy},\n  volume={22},\n  number={1},\n  pages={20},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/e22010020}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Superpixel clustering is one of the most popular computer vision techniques that aggregates coherent pixels into perceptually meaningful groups, taking inspiration from Gestalt grouping rules. However, due to brain complexity, the underlying mechanisms of such perceptual rules are unclear. Thus, conventional superpixel methods do not completely follow them and merely generate a flat image partition rather than hierarchical ones like a human does. In addition, those methods need to initialize the total number of superpixels, which may not suit diverse images. In this paper, we first propose context-aware superpixel (CASP) that follows both Gestalt grouping rules and the top-down hierarchical principle. Thus, CASP enables to adapt the total number of superpixels to specific images automatically. Next, we propose bilateral entropy, with two aspects conditional intensity entropy and spatial occupation entropy, to evaluate the encoding efficiency of image coherence. Extensive experiments demonstrate CASP achieves better superpixel segmentation performance and less entropy than baseline methods. More than that, using Pearson’s correlation coefficient, a collection of data with a total of 120 samples demonstrates a strong correlation between local image coherence and superpixel segmentation performance. Our results inversely support the reliability of above-mentioned perceptual rules, and eventually, we suggest designing novel entropy criteria to test the encoding efficiency of more complex patterns."},"title":{"value":"Context-Aware Superpixel and Bilateral Entropy - Image Coherence Induces Less Entropy"},"authors":{"value":["Feihong Liu","Xiao Zhang","Hongyu Wang","Jun Feng"]}},"tmdate":1769258244840,"pdate":1609372800000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/entropy/LiuZWF20"],"tcdate":1769258216938,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jun_Feng2"],"forum":"ljtPnhx79Y","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":794104,"cdate":1577836800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1769258244840,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ljtPnhx79Y","version":2},{"content":{"research_area_keywords":{"value":"pruning, LLM efficiency, NLP in resource-constrained settings"},"keywords":{"value":["Transformer Architecture","Attention Head Pruning","Model Stability","Attention Entropy"]},"B_use_or_create_scientific_artifacts":{"value":"No"},"languages_studied":{"value":"English"},"D4_ethics_review_board_approval":{"value":"N/A"},"C4_parameters_for_packages":{"value":"Yes"},"B6_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix D.2, Appendix D.3"},"B1_cite_creators_of_artifacts":{"value":"N/A"},"B2_discuss_the_license_for_artifacts":{"value":"N/A"},"D_human_subjects_including_annotators":{"value":"No"},"C2_elaboration":{"value":"Section 5.1, Appendix C"},"C4_elaboration":{"value":"Section 4.1"},"E_ai_assistants_in_research_or_writing":{"value":"Yes"},"C1_elaboration":{"value":"Appendix C.2, Appendix C.3"},"C2_experimental_setup_and_hyperparameters":{"value":"Yes"},"E1_elaboration":{"value":"We used AI assistants for grammar refinement, wording suggestions, and minor assistance in preparing several figures."},"C1_model_size_and_budget":{"value":"Yes"},"venue":{"value":"ACL ARR 2026 May Submission"},"D3_data_consent":{"value":"N/A"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026entropy,\ntitle={Entropy Meets Importance: A Unified Head Importance{\\textendash}Entropy Score for Stable and Efficient Transformer Pruning},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to ACL Rolling Review - May 2026},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=V64DZCBoyw},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Entropy Meets Importance: A Unified Head Importance–Entropy Score for Stable and Efficient Transformer Pruning"},"C3_descriptive_statistics":{"value":"N/A"},"contribution_types":{"value":["NLP engineering experiment","Approaches to low-compute settings (efficiency)"]},"A1_limitations_section":{"value":"This paper has a limitations section."},"B6_statistics_for_data":{"value":"Yes"},"B4_data_contains_personally_identifying_info_or_offensive_content":{"value":"N/A"},"B3_artifact_use_consistent_with_intended_use":{"value":"N/A"},"A2_potential_risks":{"value":"N/A"},"E1_information_about_use_of_ai_assistants":{"value":"Yes"},"abstract":{"value":"Transformer-based models have achieved remarkable performance. However, their structural characteristics---multiple layers and attention heads---introduce efficiency challenges in inference and deployment. To address these challenges, various pruning methods have recently been proposed. Notably, gradient-based methods using Head Importance Scores (HIS) have gained traction for interpretability, efficiency, and ability to identify redundant heads. However, HIS alone has limitations as it captures only the gradient-driven contribution, overlooking the diversity of attention patterns. To overcome these limitations, we introduce a novel pruning criterion, **HIES (Head Importance-Entropy Score)**, which integrates head importance scores with attention entropy, providing complementary evidence on per-head contribution. Empirically, HIES‐based pruning yields up to 15.2\\% improvement in model quality and $2.04\\times$ improvement in stability over HIS‐only methods, enabling substantial model compression without sacrificing either accuracy or stability. Code will be released upon publication."},"D1_instructions_given_to_participants":{"value":"N/A"},"paper_type":{"value":"Long"},"C_computational_experiments":{"value":"Yes"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/e585428fd863a7ad5b2669f18e77f2fabece4099.pdf"},"research_area":{"value":"Efficient Methods for NLP"},"B5_documentation_of_artifacts":{"value":"N/A"},"EMNLP_2026_AI_Reviewing_Experiment":{"value":"yes"},"D2_recruitment_and_payment":{"value":"N/A"},"venueid":{"value":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission"}},"tmdate":1790696940244,"tcdate":1779622658102,"writers":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission5193/Authors"],"signatures":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/Submission5193/Authors"],"forum":"V64DZCBoyw","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":5193,"cdate":1779622658102,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Edit","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Preprint_Post_Submission","aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May/-/Desk_Reject_Verification_Flag"],"mdate":1790696940244,"odate":1780385598983,"domain":"aclweb.org/ACL/ARR/2026/May","id":"V64DZCBoyw","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"NeurIPS 2025 Workshop"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/2a7adefb8ec8d3d76f10be8c5ba2b3937f19f8f9.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Transformer Architecture","Attention Head Pruning","Model Stability","Attention Entropy"]},"venueid":{"value":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT"},"paperhash":{"value":"choi|entropy_meets_importance_a_unified_head_importanceentropy_score_for_stable_and_efficient_transformer_pruning"},"authorids":{"value":["~MINSIK_CHOI1","~Hyegang_Son1","~Joohun_Hyun1","~Seokmin_Kim1","~Young_Geun_Kim1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Transformer-based models have achieved remarkable performance in NLP tasks. However, their structural characteristics—multiple layers and attention heads—introduce challenges in inference and deployment. To address these challenges, various pruning methods have recently been proposed. Notably, gradient-based methods using Head Importance Scores (HIS) have gained traction for interpretability, efficiency, and ability to identify redundant heads. However, HIS alone has limitations as it captures only the gradient-driven contribution, overlooking the diversity of attention patterns. To overcome these limitations, we introduce a novel pruning criterion, HIES (Head Importance-Entropy Score), which integrates head importance scores with attention entropy, providing complementary evidence on per-head contribution. Empirically, HIES-based pruning yields up to 17.62\\% improvement in model quality and $2.05\\times$ improvement in stability over HIS-only methods, enabling substantial model compression without sacrificing either accuracy or stability."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nchoi2025entropy,\ntitle={Entropy Meets Importance: A Unified Head Importance{\\textendash}Entropy Score for Stable and Efficient Transformer Pruning},\nauthor={MINSIK CHOI and Hyegang Son and Joohun Hyun and Seokmin Kim and Young Geun Kim},\nbooktitle={OPT 2025: Optimization for Machine Learning},\nyear={2025},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=UKx7KhS9ZV}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Entropy Meets Importance: A Unified Head Importance–Entropy Score for Stable and Efficient Transformer Pruning"},"authors":{"value":["MINSIK CHOI","Hyegang Son","Joohun Hyun","Seokmin Kim","Young Geun Kim"]}},"tmdate":1764597314550,"pdate":1758568914188,"tcdate":1756457662075,"writers":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/Submission51/Authors"],"signatures":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/Submission51/Authors"],"forum":"UKx7KhS9ZV","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":51,"cdate":1756457662075,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/-/Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/-/Post_Submission","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/-/Edit","NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT/Submission51/-/Camera_Ready"],"mdate":1764597314550,"odate":1764597314535,"domain":"NeurIPS.cc/2025/Workshop/OPT","id":"UKx7KhS9ZV","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Entropy 2012"},"pdf":{"value":"https://www.mdpi.com/1099-4300/14/11/2311/pdf?version=1424784906"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/ENTROPY/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"chen|on_the_smoothed_minimum_error_entropy_criterion"},"authorids":{"value":["~Badong_Chen1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:José_C._Príncipe:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.3390/e14112311"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/entropy/ChenP12a,\n  author={Badong Chen and José C. Príncipe},\n  title={On the Smoothed Minimum Error Entropy Criterion},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  journal={Entropy},\n  volume={14},\n  number={11},\n  pages={2311-2323},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.3390/e14112311}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent studies suggest that the minimum error entropy (MEE) criterion can outperform the traditional mean square error criterion in supervised machine learning, especially in nonlinear and non-Gaussian situations. In practice, however, one has to estimate the error entropy from the samples since in general the analytical evaluation of error entropy is not possible. By the Parzen windowing approach, the estimated error entropy converges asymptotically to the entropy of the error plus an independent random variable whose probability density function (PDF) corresponds to the kernel function in the Parzen method. This quantity of entropy is called the smoothed error entropy, and the corresponding optimality criterion is named the smoothed MEE (SMEE) criterion. In this paper, we study theoretically the SMEE criterion in supervised machine learning where the learning machine is assumed to be nonparametric and universal. Some basic properties are presented. In particular, we show that when the smoothing factor is very small, the smoothed error entropy equals approximately the true error entropy plus a scaled version of the Fisher information of error. We also investigate how the smoothing factor affects the optimal solution. In some special situations, the optimal solution under the SMEE criterion does not change with increasing smoothing factor. In general cases, when the smoothing factor tends to infinity, minimizing the smoothed error entropy will be approximately equivalent to minimizing error variance, regardless of the conditional PDF and the kernel."},"title":{"value":"On the Smoothed Minimum Error Entropy Criterion"},"authors":{"value":["Badong Chen","José C. Príncipe"]}},"tmdate":1768983014670,"pdate":1356912000000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/entropy/ChenP12a"],"tcdate":1768982935913,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Badong_Chen1"],"forum":"JcToCZdS7P","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":781025,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1768983014670,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"JcToCZdS7P","version":2}],"count":10000}